### What is the difference between HashMap, LinkedHashMap, and TreeMap?

All three implement the `Map` interface but differ in ordering, performance, and internal structure.

| Feature | HashMap | LinkedHashMap | TreeMap |
|---|---|---|---|
| Internal structure | Hash table | Hash table + linked list | Red-Black tree |
| Ordering | None | Insertion or access order | Sorted (natural/Comparator) |
| get/put | O(1) | O(1) | O(log n) |
| Null key | 1 allowed | 1 allowed | Not allowed |
| Null values | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| NavigableMap | No | No | Yes |
| Memory | Lowest | Medium (+pointers) | Highest (+tree nodes) |
| Thread-safe | No | No | No |

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class MapComparisonDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, BigDecimal> hashMap = new HashMap<>();
        Map<String, BigDecimal> linkedMap = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        Map<String, BigDecimal> treeMap = new TreeMap<>();

        // Insert in this order: CUR, SAV, FD
        for (var m : List.of(hashMap, linkedMap, treeMap)) {
            m.put("CUR3001", new BigDecimal("75000"));
            m.put("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("150000"));
            m.put("FD4001", new BigDecimal("500000"));
        }

        System.out.println("HashMap:       " + hashMap.keySet());
        System.out.println("LinkedHashMap: " + linkedMap.keySet());
        System.out.println("TreeMap:       " + treeMap.keySet());
    }
}
```

```text
HashMap:       [FD4001, CUR3001, SAV2001]
LinkedHashMap: [CUR3001, SAV2001, FD4001]
TreeMap:       [CUR3001, FD4001, SAV2001]
```

### How do you build an LRU cache with LinkedHashMap?

LinkedHashMap supports access-order mode: when you call `get()` or `put()` on an existing key, the entry moves to the end of the linked list. Combined with `removeEldestEntry()`, this creates an LRU (Least Recently Used) cache.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class LRUCacheDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // LRU cache: max 3 entries, access-order
        Map<String, BigDecimal> cache = new LinkedHashMap<>(16, 0.75f, true) {
            @Override
            protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<String, BigDecimal> eldest) {
                return size() > 3; // evict when more than 3 entries
            }
        };

        cache.put("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("150000"));
        cache.put("CUR3001", new BigDecimal("75000"));
        cache.put("FD4001", new BigDecimal("500000"));
        System.out.println("Initial: " + cache.keySet());
        // [SAV2001, CUR3001, FD4001]

        // Access SAV2001 → moves to end
        cache.get("SAV2001");
        System.out.println("After get(SAV): " + cache.keySet());
        // [CUR3001, FD4001, SAV2001]

        // Add new entry → evicts CUR3001 (eldest/least recently used)
        cache.put("LON5001", new BigDecimal("200000"));
        System.out.println("After put(LON): " + cache.keySet());
        // [FD4001, SAV2001, LON5001]
    }
}
```

```text
Initial: [SAV2001, CUR3001, FD4001]
After get(SAV): [CUR3001, FD4001, SAV2001]
After put(LON): [FD4001, SAV2001, LON5001]
```

### What are TreeMap's NavigableMap operations?

TreeMap implements `NavigableMap`, providing powerful range query methods:

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class NavigableMapDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TreeMap<String, BigDecimal> accounts = new TreeMap<>();
        accounts.put("CUR3001", new BigDecimal("75000"));
        accounts.put("FD4001", new BigDecimal("500000"));
        accounts.put("LON5001", new BigDecimal("200000"));
        accounts.put("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("150000"));
        accounts.put("SAV2002", new BigDecimal("80000"));

        System.out.println("First: " + accounts.firstKey());     // CUR3001
        System.out.println("Last: " + accounts.lastKey());       // SAV2002
        System.out.println("Floor(G): " + accounts.floorKey("G")); // FD4001 (<=G)
        System.out.println("Ceiling(G): " + accounts.ceilingKey("G")); // LON5001 (>=G)

        // Range queries
        System.out.println("Sub [CUR..SAV): " + accounts.subMap("CUR3001", "SAV2001"));
        System.out.println("Head (<LON): " + accounts.headMap("LON5001"));
        System.out.println("Tail (>=LON): " + accounts.tailMap("LON5001"));

        // Descending order
        System.out.println("Descending: " + accounts.descendingMap().keySet());
    }
}
```

```text
First: CUR3001
Last: SAV2002
Floor(G): FD4001
Ceiling(G): LON5001
Sub [CUR..SAV): {CUR3001=75000, FD4001=500000, LON5001=200000}
Head (<LON): {CUR3001=75000, FD4001=500000}
Tail (>=LON): {LON5001=200000, SAV2001=150000, SAV2002=80000}
Descending: [SAV2002, SAV2001, LON5001, FD4001, CUR3001]
```

### When do you use each?

| Use case | Best choice | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Fast key-value lookup, order irrelevant | HashMap | O(1), lowest memory |
| Preserve insertion order | LinkedHashMap | O(1), deterministic iteration |
| LRU cache | LinkedHashMap (access-order) | O(1) with automatic eviction |
| Sorted keys | TreeMap | O(log n), NavigableMap |
| Range queries ("accounts between A and F") | TreeMap | subMap, headMap, tailMap |
| JSON serialization with consistent order | LinkedHashMap | Insertion order = field order |
| Thread-safe map | ConcurrentHashMap | Per-bucket locking |
| Thread-safe sorted map | ConcurrentSkipListMap | Lock-free, sorted |

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake 1: Using HashMap when output order matters

```java
// WRONG: JSON output order is unpredictable
Map<String, String> response = new HashMap<>();
response.put("status", "OK");
response.put("account", "SAV2001");
// Serialized: {"account":"SAV2001","status":"OK"} – random order!

// CORRECT: use LinkedHashMap for predictable field order
Map<String, String> response = new LinkedHashMap<>();
response.put("status", "OK");
response.put("account", "SAV2001");
// Serialized: {"status":"OK","account":"SAV2001"} – insertion order!
```

#### Mistake 2: Using TreeMap when you only need sorted output once

```java
// SUBOPTIMAL: TreeMap for one-time sort (O(n log n) on every put)
TreeMap<String, String> sorted = new TreeMap<>(dataMap);

// BETTER: HashMap for storage, sort once when needed
Map<String, String> fast = new HashMap<>(dataMap);
List<String> sortedKeys = new ArrayList<>(fast.keySet());
Collections.sort(sortedKeys); // one-time O(n log n)
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **LinkedHashMap.removeEldestEntry is called on every put.** If it returns true, the eldest entry is removed. This is the hook for building caches with bounded size.

- **TreeMap.subMap, headMap, tailMap return views.** Modifications to the view affect the original TreeMap. Use `new TreeMap<>(map.subMap(from, to))` for an independent copy.

- **ConcurrentSkipListMap.** The thread-safe sorted map equivalent of TreeMap. It uses a skip list instead of a Red-Black tree and supports NavigableMap.

- **EnumMap.** When keys are enum constants, use `EnumMap` instead of HashMap. It uses an array indexed by enum ordinal – faster and more memory-efficient than any hash-based map.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you implement an LRU cache?" – LinkedHashMap with accessOrder=true + removeEldestEntry.
- "What is the time complexity of TreeMap operations?" – O(log n) for get/put/remove.
- "Can TreeMap have null keys?" – No, compareTo/Comparator cannot handle null.
- "What is NavigableMap?" – Extended SortedMap with floor/ceiling/lower/higher and range operations.
